#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The voice database: every text of the game that gets a voice, with what is needed to voice it well.
How it is made and used: Doc/guides/voice-generation.md. Not the game's data framework, and never
shipped: it is the workbench for the voice overs (Doc/ideas/Lore.md, *Voice*).

Reads:
  staging        the texts and their speakers, in the staging format (Doc/ideas/Data.md, *Staging*):
                 dev/voices/staging/*.jsonl (not in git), or the files given
  dev/voices/cast.md         the voice presets, one per kind of speaker
  dev/voices/names.md        how lore names are said
  dev/voices/fixes.jsonl     hand fixes: {"key": "npc:448", "preset": "gnoll-male"}, or a line's "voice": false
  dev/voices/analysis/*.jsonl  direction for single lines, by a language model or by hand, tied to the
                 text's hash: {"key": "quest:176:offer#1", "hash": "...", "tone": ..., "direction": ...}
Writes (dev/voices/build/, not in git; made again by every build):
  lines.jsonl    one line per part of a text that is voiced, with its speaker, preset, analysis, request
  voices.sqlite  the same as tables, for questions (sqlite3 dev/voices/build/voices.sqlite)

Usage:  dev/tools/voicedb build [staging.jsonl ...]   make the database
        dev/tools/voicedb stats                        what is in it: counts, characters, cost, presets
        dev/tools/voicedb show <key or words>          entries in full (a key like quest:176:offer#1,
                                                       or words found in the text or the speaker)
        dev/tools/voicedb request <key>                what would be sent to the voice service
        dev/tools/voicedb batch <file> [filter]        lines for a language model to direct (see the guide)
        dev/tools/voicedb names [n]                    names in the texts that names.md lacks
        dev/tools/voicedb check                        cast.md and names.md read without errors
"""
